{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension compared\ncase-insensitively. A source with any syntax error MUST fail to parse, reporting\nthe line of its first error. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no gaps. Every obligation has implementing code carrying an {impl} reference and a test.\n\n**Obligations, code and tests**\n\n- **Seven languages parsed with pinned grammars, selected by extension case-insensitively.**\n  - Code: `profile::profiles` and `profile::profile_for_path` (lowercases the extension), the per-language `PROFILE` statics, and `outline::parse_redacted`.\n  - The `Cargo.toml` tree-sitter dependencies are pinned exactly (`=x.y.z`).\n  - Test: `profiles_are_selected_by_extension_case_insensitively` covers `.RS`, `.Py`, `.TSX`, `.MD`, `.TOML`, and plain-text fallbacks for no extension and `.rs.txt`.\n  - Other tests exercise the Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML profiles.\n- **A syntax error fails the parse and reports the line of the first error.**\n  - Code: `check_errors` (a depth-first search for the first error or missing node), `OutlineError`, and its `Display` (`line N: …`).\n  - Test: `reports_parse_failures_with_lines` checks Rust line 2, a Python failure, and TOML where the first of two errors, on line 3, is reported.\n  - The check is language-independent, so covering three languages is enough.\n- **Plain-text profile: each non-blank line is an anonymous `line` node.**\n  - Code: `plain_text` and `plain::PROFILE`.\n  - Tests: `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` and `outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs`.\n- **Each profile maps grammar kinds to short kind names, with a naming rule (field, child, custom, or anonymous).**\n  - Code: `KindRule`, `NameRule`, `Profile`, `Profile::rule`, the per-language `KINDS` tables, and the `Builder::name` helper.\n  - Tests: the per-profile tests, which assert kinds and names for Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, YAML, and Rust (the last through `line_queries_find_innermost_nodes` and the tools test).\n- **Only mapped kinds appear; other nodes are transparent.**\n  - Code: `Builder::walk` recurses into unmapped nodes with the same parent.\n  - Tests: the TypeScript `export` wrapper is transparent in `typescript_profile_outlines_declarations`. `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` checks that unmapped statements add no nodes.\n- **Profiles MAY attach siblings.** (`MAY` imposes nothing to check.)\n  - Code: the `attaches` functions for Rust and Python, and `finish`, which computes the line range from attached siblings.\n  - Tests: Python decorator and comment line range `2-5` in `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements`. The Rust attribute and doc-comment line range is checked in the hashes test, which has other {test} references.\n- **Every outline has a root node for the whole file, and unmapped source belongs to the root with no fallback kinds.**\n  - Code: `root_node` and `walk`.\n  - Tests: `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root`, `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds`, and `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements`, which checks that a top-level import and `if` map to the root.\n- **Comments are not outline nodes.**\n  - Code: `Builder::maps` excludes `is_comment` nodes, and `walk` records comments separately.\n  - Tests: the CSS, YAML, TOML, and Markdown `summary` assertions list no comment nodes, and the Markdown test asserts one recorded comment.\n- **Profiles are documented.** `md/reference.md`, \"Outline profiles\", has a table of kinds, grammar nodes, and names for every profile, with details per language.\n- **Upgrading a grammar or profile is a deliberate change.** The exact `=` pins in `Cargo.toml` and the documentation paragraph cover this. The `MAY` part imposes nothing to check.\n\n**Marking.** Test functions and helpers carry {test}. Code implementing the requirement carries {impl}. The Rust-only naming helpers (`impl_name`, `extern_name`, `item_macro`, `in_item_list`) carry {impl} to the more specific `requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`, which governs exactly those decisions. I do not count that as a marking gap. Markdown's `is_comment` is marked for `outline_comments` in the same way.\n\n**Unspecified behavior.** None found. The \"missing X\" wording in syntax-error messages falls within \"reporting the line of its first error\".",
